# ✨ Godot 4 GPU Particles & Visual Effects (VFX) Mastery

This skill provides the architectural foundation and components for building juice-packed **2D & 3D GPU Particle Systems** in Godot 4.x.

---

## ⚡ 1. GPUParticles Architecture & Lifecycle

Always prefer `GPUParticles2D`/`GPUParticles3D` over CPU particles for vastly superior rendering performance.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Emitter["GPUParticles2D / 3D\n(one_shot: true, exploding: true)"] --> Mat["ParticleProcessMaterial\n(Direction, Velocity, Turbulence)"]
    Mat --> SubAtCol["Sub-Emitter: AT_COLLISION\n(Sparks on floor/walls)"]
    Mat --> SubAtEnd["Sub-Emitter: AT_END\n(Smoke puff on particle death)"]
    Emitter --> Cleanup["Signal: finished -> queue_free() or return to pool"]
```

### 💎 Key GPU Particle Invariants:
1. **One-Shot Auto Cleanup**: Always connect the `finished` signal to either `queue_free()` or return the emitter to an object pool.
2. **Sub-Emitters**: Godot 4 supports GPU sub-emitters. Create the parent emitter, then assign the sub-emitter node in the `sub_emitter` property of the `ParticleProcessMaterial`.
3. **Turbulence**: Use GPU Particle Turbulence for organic fluid/smoke motion without CPU overhead.

